 ('video-aspect-ratio-guide','Vertical, square, or widescreen video: how to choose','Choose the frame from the destination and the visual idea.','Choose vertical for phone-first shorts|A 9:16 frame gives a tall canvas; keep labels and action in a safe area.','Choose square for balanced compositions|A 1:1 frame works when the subject is central and portable across placements.','Choose widescreen for horizontal relationships|A 16:9 frame gives room for maps, comparisons, presentations, and wide scenes.','Design for the crop|Keep essential information away from edges and preview at actual size.','Check current platform rules|Specifications and layouts change; confirm before publishing.'),
 ('how-to-make-history-shorts','How to make a short animated history video','Turn one historical moment into a focused, accurate story.','Choose one event or change|Pick an invention, journey, decision, or turning point.','Set the place and time|Give enough context to understand who is acting and what problem exists.','Build a three-part sequence|Show the situation, turning point, and why the change mattered.','Separate evidence from interpretation|Use careful wording for disputed or simplified claims.','Verify before publishing|Check names, dates, locations, translations, images, and rights.'),
 ('faceless-educational-video-guide','How to make faceless educational videos','Teach a focused lesson with animation and narration instead of a presenter.','Write one learning objective|Use “After watching, the learner can…” and keep it observable.','Choose the example|A familiar object, process, or comparison can carry the explanation.','Sequence the explanation|Introduce parts, show their relationship, then restate the idea.','Design for access|Use readable text, clear narration, contrast, and appropriate pacing.','Review the evidence|Check sources and terminology, especially for high-stakes subjects.'),
 ('animated-mystery-story-ideas','How to make an animated mystery short','Use one clue, one question, and one reveal.','Open with an anomaly|Show the clock that stops, shadow that moves, or book that changes places.','Give the question a direction|Make possible explanations distinct enough for a theory.','Place fair clues|Let the audience see details that make the ending plausible.','Reveal with an action|Show the answer through what a character or object does.','Review the promise|Make sure the ending answers the opening question and matches the tone.'),
 ('visual-comparison-video-guide','How to make a visual comparison video','Put both sides on the same basis so the difference is understood.','Define the comparison|Use the same unit, period, condition, or scale.','Give context|Use an object, distance, timeline, or repeated visual unit to make scale visible.','Keep the grammar consistent|Use the same labels, colors, camera, and measurement logic.','Explain the reason|Give the cause or implication, not only “A is bigger.”','Verify every figure|Check measurements, dates, definitions, and rounding; label estimates.'),
 ('animation-brief-examples','Animated video brief examples','Practical briefs for explainers, history, stories, quizzes, and visual facts.','Explainer brief|Explain why the Moon can be visible during the day to a beginner; show the Earth, Moon, and daylight; end with one verified takeaway.','History brief|Follow a trade route on a map; introduce places, show what moved, and explain why it mattered; verify the details.','Story brief|A lamp helps a seedling through a rainy night; give the lamp a goal, obstacle, and hopeful action.','Quiz brief|Ask whether light or sound travels faster; give time to guess, reveal, and explain the lightning example.','Visual-fact brief|Compare two familiar objects using one unit and a clear scale; state what the comparison does and does not prove.'),
